{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01ALkNw67MpW1cZXeGFHEffr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013PH9e9SF6yW7bo3pV6C31P","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/structfuse-context/artifacts/code/training/contrastive_model.py"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15859,"cache_read_input_tokens":46511,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15859,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0173ChWZFKfkzx686zBjgLNV","session_id":"ef1befa8-2efd-49dd-aab6-f69049f8448e","uuid":"9bcb08e7-25ed-4d19-8e5c-64ad6d8c92ef"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vc3Ckq4bZnfHUdD5ZWKskW","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/structfuse-context/memory.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Inference-Time Graph Methods 文献对比\\\\n\\\\n## 对比表\\\\n\\\\n| Method | Paper | Venue | Graph Type | Usage Stage | Inter-proc Data-Flow | Task | Scalability | Key Diff from StructFuse |\\\\n|--------|-------|-------|-----------|-------------|---------------------|------|-------------|--------------------------|\\\\n| **CodexGraph** | Bridging LLMs and Code Repositories via Code Graph Databases | NAACL 2025 (arXiv:2408.03910) | Code graph DB (MODULE/CLASS/FUNCTION nodes; CONTAINS/INHERITS/USES edges) | Inference-time | No. Static analysis for symbol relations only | General repo-level coding (completion, issue resolution) | CrossCodeEval, SWE-bench, EvoCodeBench | Agent queries graph DB at inference; task-agnostic schema but no data-flow semantics |\\\\n| **LocAgent** | Graph-Guided LLM Agents for Code Localization | ACL 2025 (arXiv:2503.09089) | Directed heterogeneous graph (code entities + dependency edges) | Inference-time | No. Dependency edges, not data-flow | Fault localization | 92.7% file-level accuracy; SWE-bench +12% Pass@10 | Agent multi-hop graph reasoning at inference; needs fine-tuned 32B model |\\\\n| **RANGER** | Repository-Level Agent for Graph-Enhanced Retrieval | arXiv:2509.25257 (Sep 2025) | Knowledge graph + MCTS traversal | Inference-time | No | Code search, QA, cross-file retrieval, completion | CodeSearchNet, RepoQA, RepoBench, CrossCodeEval | MCTS-guided graph exploration; dual-stage (Cypher + MCTS); heavy inference cost |\\\\n| **RepoGraph** | Enhancing AI SE with Repository-level Code Graph | ICLR 2025 (arXiv:2410.14684) | Repo-level code graph (class hierarchy, call graph, file structure) | Inference-time | No. Structural relations only | Software engineering / issue resolution | SWE-bench (+32.8% relative); CrossCodeEval | Plug-in module for existing agents; repo-wide navigation; no semantic analysis |\\\\n| **GraphCoder** | Enhancing Repo-Level Code Completion via Code Context Graph | ASE 2024 (arXiv:2406.07003) | Code Context Graph (CFG + CDG + DDG) | Inference-time | Partial. Data dependence graph but **intra-procedural** only | Code completion | Repository-level benchmarks | Coarse-to-fine retrieval with CCG; closest to StructFuse in using data/control flow, but intra-proc and at inference-time |\\\\n| **CodeRAG** | Supportive Code Retrieval on Bigraph for Real-World Code Generation | arXiv:2504.10046 (Apr 2025) | Bigraph (requirement graph + DS-code graph) | Inference-time | No. Dependency + semantic edges | Repo-level code generation | DevEval (+40.90 Pass@1 w/ GPT-4o) | Dual-graph (requirements + code); agentic reasoning; high inference overhead |\\\\n| **ARISE** | Repository-level Graph for Agentic Fault Localization and Program Repair | arXiv:2605.03117 (May 2025) | Multi-granularity program graph (statement nodes + def-use edges) | Inference-time | Partial. **Intra-procedural** def-use edges + data-flow slicing tool | Fault localization + repair | SWE-bench Lite (300 issues); +17.0 Function Recall@1 | Closest to StructFuse in data-flow usage; but intra-proc only, agent queries at inference |\\\\n| **CodeGRAG** | Bridging NL and PL via Graphical RAG | arXiv:2405.02355 (2024) | Composed syntax graph (AST + DFG + CFG) | Both (GNN training + graph prompt at inference) | Partial. DFG extraction but function-level | Code generation (cross-lingual) | Multiple PLs | Only method with training-time component (GNN soft prompts); but graph also used at inference |\\\\n| **StructFuse** (Ours) | Structure-Grounded Hard Negatives via CodeQL Data-Flow | — | Inter-procedural data-flow graph (CodeQL taint tracking) | **Training-time only** | **Yes. Full inter-procedural** (source→sink across call boundaries) | Code retrieval (contrastive training) | Large-scale repos with CodeQL | Zero inference overhead; graph used offline for negative mining; inter-proc data-flow |\\\\n\\\\n## Discussion 要点\\\\n\\\\n### StructFuse vs Inference-Time Graph Methods: 互补性与差异性\\\\n\\\\n1. **Cost-accuracy tradeoff**: Inference-time methods (CodexGraph, RANGER, RepoGraph) achieve high precision by constructing and querying graphs per request, but incur significant latency (graph construction + traversal + LLM calls). StructFuse amortizes all graph computation to training time—once the encoder is trained with structure-grounded negatives, inference is a single embedding lookup with zero graph overhead. This makes StructFuse suitable for latency-sensitive production retrieval where inference-time graph methods are impractical.\\\\n\\\\n2. **Data-flow depth**: Most inference-time methods use structural graphs (call graphs, class hierarchies, file dependencies). Only GraphCoder and ARISE incorporate data/control flow, but limited to **intra-procedural** scope. StructFuse uniquely leverages **inter-procedural** data-flow via CodeQL's taint tracking, capturing cross-function semantic relationships (e.g., a sanitizer function that transforms data between source and sink across call boundaries). This deeper semantic signal generates harder, more informative negatives.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Complementarity**: StructFuse and inference-time methods are orthogonal and composable. A StructFuse-trained encoder can serve as the embedding backbone for systems like RANGER or CodexGraph, providing better initial retrieval that reduces the graph agent's search space. Conversely, inference-time graph methods can provide reranking on top of StructFuse retrieval for tasks requiring maximum precision.\\\\n\\\\n4. **Scalability model**: Inference-time methods scale linearly with query volume (each query triggers graph operations). StructFuse's cost is front-loaded: CodeQL analysis runs once per repository update, and the resulting negatives improve the encoder permanently. For high-QPS code search services, this one-time investment yields compounding returns.\\\\n\\\\n5. **Task generality vs specialization**: Inference-time graph agents (CodexGraph, RepoGraph) are task-agnostic but require powerful LLMs as backends. StructFuse produces a specialized retrieval encoder that excels at the code search task without LLM inference, making it deployable as a lightweight, efficient first-stage retriever in multi-stage pipelines.\\\\n\\\\n## Related Work 段落草稿 (~200 words)\\\\n\\\\n**Graph-Based Methods for Code Understanding.**\\\\nRecent work has explored leveraging code graphs to enhance LLM-based software engineering. Inference-time approaches construct and query graphs during each interaction: CodexGraph (Liu et al., NAACL 2025) builds a code graph database that LLM agents query via structured interfaces; RepoGraph (Ouyang et al., ICLR 2025) provides repository-level navigation as a plug-in module for SE agents; LocAgent (ACL 2025) and RANGER (2025) employ graph-guided multi-hop reasoning and MCTS-based traversal for code localization and retrieval, respectively. GraphCoder (ASE 2024) incorporates intra-procedural control-flow and data-dependence graphs for code completion retrieval, while ARISE (2025) extends this with statement-level def-use edges for fault localization. CodeGRAG (2024) bridges training and inference by using GNN-encoded graph prompts.\\\\n\\\\nThese methods achieve strong results but impose substantial inference-time costs—graph construction, traversal, and often multiple LLM calls per query. Moreover, their data-flow analysis remains intra-procedural. StructFuse takes a fundamentally different approach: we leverage CodeQL's inter-procedural taint tracking to generate structure-grounded hard negatives at **training time**, producing an encoder that captures cross-function data-flow semantics without any graph overhead at inference. This positions StructFuse as complementary to inference-time graph methods—it can serve as an efficient first-stage retriever whose embeddings already encode deep structural relationships that other methods must compute on-the-fly.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 参考文献\\\\n\\\\n- CodexGraph: arXiv:2408.03910, NAACL 2025\\\\n- LocAgent: arXiv:2503.09089, ACL 2025\\\\n- RANGER: arXiv:2509.25257, 2025\\\\n- RepoGraph: arXiv:2410.14684, ICLR 2025\\\\n- GraphCoder: arXiv:2406.07003, ASE 2024\\\\n- CodeRAG: arXiv:2504.10046, 2025\\\\n- ARISE: arXiv:2605.03117, 2025\\\\n- CodeGRAG: arXiv:2405.02355, 2024\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 8062, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KqFxUgrzMKjo6CxzEJx7g1","session_id":"ef1befa8-2efd-49dd-aab6-f69049f8448e","uuid":"78a2239e-f77f-46a2-9156-833d905ba559","timestamp":"2026-05-14T17:00:24.586Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013PH9e9SF6yW7bo3pV6C31P","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"StructFuse contrastive bi-encoder with fusion head.\n2\t\n3\tBi-encoder over Qwen2.5-Coder-3B with InfoNCE loss, weighted positives\n4\t(data-flow path distance), and an optional lightweight fusion head that\n5\tcombines embedding similarity with explicit structural features.\n6\t\"\"\"\n7\t\n8\timport math\n9\timport torch\n10\timport torch.nn as nn\n11\timport torch.nn.functional as F\n12\tfrom transformers import AutoModel, AutoConfig\n13\t\n14\t\n15\tclass FusionHead(nn.Module):\n16\t    \"\"\"MLP that fuses embedding similarity with explicit features into a relevance score.\"\"\"\n17\t\n18\t    FEATURE_NAMES = [\n19\t        \"dataflow_distance\",\n20\t        \"bm25_score\",\n21\t        \"co_change_freq\",\n22\t        \"inter_procedural_flag\",\n23\t        \"path_count\",\n24\t    ]\n25\t    NUM_EXPLICIT = len(FEATURE_NAMES)\n26\t\n27\t    def __init__(self, hidden_dim: int = 128):\n28\t        super().__init__()\n29\t        input_dim = 1 + self.NUM_EXPLICIT  # embed_similarity + explicit features\n30\t        self.net = nn.Sequential(\n31\t            nn.Linear(input_dim, hidden_dim),\n32\t            nn.GELU(),\n33\t            nn.Linear(hidden_dim, hidden_dim),\n34\t            nn.GELU(),\n35\t            nn.Linear(hidden_dim, 1),\n36\t        )\n37\t\n38\t    def forward(self, embed_similarity: torch.Tensor, features: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n39\t        \"\"\"\n40\t        Args:\n41\t            embed_similarity: (B,) or (B, K) cosine similarities.\n42\t            features: same leading dims + NUM_EXPLICIT trailing dim.\n43\t        Returns:\n44\t            scores with same shape as embed_similarity.\n45\t        \"\"\"\n46\t        orig_shape = embed_similarity.shape\n47\t        sim = embed_similarity.unsqueeze(-1)  # (..., 1)\n48\t        x = torch.cat([sim, features], dim=-1)  # (..., 1+NUM_EXPLICIT)\n49\t        x = x.view(-1, x.size(-1))\n50\t        out = self.net(x).squeeze(-1)\n51\t        return out.view(orig_shape)\n52\t\n53\t\n54\tclass StructFuseEncoder(nn.Module):\n55\t    \"\"\"Bi-encoder with shared Qwen backbone, optional projection & LoRA.\"\"\"\n56\t\n57\t    def __init__(\n58\t        self,\n59\t        model_name: str = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-Coder-3B\",\n60\t        proj_dim: int = 256,\n61\t        use_projection: bool = True,\n62\t        use_lora: bool = False,\n63\t        lora_r: int = 16,\n64\t        lora_alpha: int = 32,\n65\t        use_fusion_head: bool = False,\n66\t        fusion_hidden: int = 128,\n67\t    ):\n68\t        super().__init__()\n69\t        self.backbone = AutoModel.from_pretrained(model_name, trust_remote_code=True)\n70\t        hidden_size = self.backbone.config.hidden_size\n71\t\n72\t        self.use_projection = use_projection\n73\t        if use_projection:\n74\t            self.projection = nn.Sequential(\n75\t                nn.Linear(hidden_size, hidden_size),\n76\t                nn.GELU(),\n77\t                nn.Linear(hidden_size, proj_dim),\n78\t            )\n79\t            self.embed_dim = proj_dim\n80\t        else:\n81\t            self.projection = None\n82\t            self.embed_dim = hidden_size\n83\t\n84\t        self.use_fusion_head = use_fusion_head\n85\t        if use_fusion_head:\n86\t            self.fusion_head = FusionHead(hidden_dim=fusion_hidden)\n87\t\n88\t        # learnable temperature for InfoNCE\n89\t        self.log_temperature = nn.Parameter(torch.tensor(math.log(0.07)))\n90\t\n91\t        if use_lora:\n92\t            self._apply_lora(lora_r, lora_alpha)\n93\t\n94\t    def _apply_lora(self, r: int, alpha: int):\n95\t        from peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model\n96\t\n97\t        lora_cfg = LoraConfig(\n98\t            r=r,\n99\t            lora_alpha=alpha,\n100\t            target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"v_proj\"],\n101\t            lora_dropout=0.05,\n102\t            bias=\"none\",\n103\t            task_type=\"FEATURE_EXTRACTION\",\n104\t        )\n105\t        self.backbone = get_peft_model(self.backbone, lora_cfg)\n106\t\n107\t    def enable_gradient_checkpointing(self):\n108\t        self.backbone.gradient_checkpointing_enable()\n109\t\n110\t    @property\n111\t    def temperature(self) -> torch.Tensor:\n112\t        return self.log_temperature.exp()\n113\t\n114\t    def encode(self, input_ids: torch.Tensor, attention_mask: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n115\t        \"\"\"Mean-pool last hidden states (excluding padding) and optionally project.\"\"\"\n116\t        outputs = self.backbone(input_ids=input_ids, attention_mask=attention_mask)\n117\t        hidden = outputs.last_hidden_state  # (B, L, H)\n118\t\n119\t        mask_expanded = attention_mask.unsqueeze(-1).float()  # (B, L, 1)\n120\t        summed = (hidden * mask_expanded).sum(dim=1)  # (B, H)\n121\t        lengths = mask_expanded.sum(dim=1).clamp(min=1e-9)  # (B, 1)\n122\t        pooled = summed / lengths  # (B, H)\n123\t\n124\t        if self.projection is not None:\n125\t            pooled = self.projection(pooled)\n126\t\n127\t        return F.normalize(pooled, p=2, dim=-1)\n128\t\n129\t    def forward(\n130\t        self,\n131\t        query_ids: torch.Tensor,\n132\t        query_mask: torch.Tensor,\n133\t        pos_ids: torch.Tensor,\n134\t        pos_mask: torch.Tensor,\n135\t        neg_ids: torch.Tensor,\n136\t        neg_mask: torch.Tensor,\n137\t        pos_weights: torch.Tensor | None = None,\n138\t        features: torch.Tensor | None = None,\n139\t    ) -> tuple[torch.Tensor, torch.Tensor]:\n140\t        \"\"\"\n141\t        Compute InfoNCE loss with weighted positives and optional fusion head.\n142\t\n143\t        Args:\n144\t            query_ids/mask: (B, Lq)\n145\t            pos_ids/mask:   (B, Lp)       — one positive per query\n146\t            neg_ids/mask:   (B, K, Ln)    — K explicit negatives per query\n147\t            pos_weights:    (B,)          — exp(-alpha * path_distance) weights\n148\t            features:       (B, 1+K, NUM_EXPLICIT) — features for pos + negs if fusion head is used\n149\t\n150\t        Returns:\n151\t            loss: scalar\n152\t            logits: (B, 1+K) similarity scores (for monitoring)\n153\t        \"\"\"\n154\t        B = query_ids.size(0)\n155\t        K = neg_ids.size(1)\n156\t\n157\t        q_emb = self.encode(query_ids, query_mask)  # (B, D)\n158\t        p_emb = self.encode(pos_ids, pos_mask)  # (B, D)\n159\t\n160\t        # flatten negatives for encoding, then reshape back\n161\t        neg_ids_flat = neg_ids.view(B * K, -1)\n162\t        neg_mask_flat = neg_mask.view(B * K, -1)\n163\t        n_emb = self.encode(neg_ids_flat, neg_mask_flat).view(B, K, -1)  # (B, K, D)\n164\t\n165\t        # cosine similarities\n166\t        pos_sim = (q_emb * p_emb).sum(dim=-1)  # (B,)\n167\t        neg_sim = torch.bmm(n_emb, q_emb.unsqueeze(-1)).squeeze(-1)  # (B, K)\n168\t\n169\t        if self.use_fusion_head and features is not None:\n170\t            all_sim = torch.cat([pos_sim.unsqueeze(1), neg_sim], dim=1)  # (B, 1+K)\n171\t            logits = self.fusion_head(all_sim, features) / self.temperature\n172\t        else:\n173\t            logits = torch.cat([pos_sim.unsqueeze(1), neg_sim], dim=1) / self.temperature  # (B, 1+K)\n174\t\n175\t        # InfoNCE: labels are always index 0 (the positive)\n176\t        labels = torch.zeros(B, dtype=torch.long, device=logits.device)\n177\t\n178\t        if pos_weights is not None:\n179\t            # Weight per-sample loss, not logits — scaling logits inverts the intended effect\n180\t            per_sample_loss = F.cross_entropy(logits, labels, reduction='none')\n181\t            loss = (per_sample_loss * pos_weights).mean()\n182\t        else:\n183\t            loss = F.cross_entropy(logits, labels)\n184\t\n185\t        return loss, logits\n186\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0173ChWZFKfkzx686zBjgLNV","session_id":"ef1befa8-2efd-49dd-aab6-f69049f8448e","uuid":"225ee27f-55d9-4b2a-875e-7ebac39dc84d","timestamp":"2026-05-14T17:00:24.587Z"}
